.TH "BCR" "1" "" "" "Breadcrumb SDD"
.SH "NAME"
.PP
bcr \- audit the breadcrumbs of a repository
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract [FILE...]
bcr verify
bcr audit
bcr report
bcr filter [\-i ID] [\-o ID] [\-t TYPE] [\-k KIND]
bcr init [\-a FILE]
.fi
.RE
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBbcr\fR is the toolkit of Breadcrumb. Four of its commands are the stages of a pipe:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit | bcr report
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBbcr extract\fR reads the breadcrumbs of a repository from the front matter of its files and prints them as records. \fBbcr verify\fR checks the set of breadcrumbs, and its shape when the repository declares one in \fBbreadcrumb.rules\fR, \fBbcr audit\fR checks their claims against the repository's files, and \fBbcr report\fR writes a page of what the pipe found. Each stage reads the records of the one before it from standard input. \fBbcr filter\fR prints only the records that match its flags, wherever records flow. \fBbcr init\fR sets a repository up for the pipe. Run \fBbcr\fR from the root of the repository.
.PP
This document is the user documentation of \fBbcr\fR: its commands, flags, inputs, outputs and exit codes. Where it and a command's spec disagree, the spec is right (ADR\-0008).
.PP
Every command follows the same conventions (ADR\-0006):
.IP \(bu 2
It is run as \fBbcr COMMAND [FLAGS] [OPERANDS]\fR. Flags come before operands; the first operand, or \fB\-\-\fR, ends them.
.IP \(bu 2
Flags are in getopt style: a short flag is one letter, such as \fB\-a\fR, and short flags can be combined, as in \fB\-ab\fR; a long flag is a name, such as \fB\-\-agents\-file\fR. A flag's value follows it: \fB\-x VALUE\fR, \fB\-xVALUE\fR, \fB\-\-name=VALUE\fR or \fB\-\-name VALUE\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Results go to standard output, and nothing else does. Messages go to standard error and start with \fBbcr: \fR. A usage error is followed by the usage line.
.IP \(bu 2
The exit status says whether the command found something wrong (see EXIT STATUS).
.SH "COMMANDS"
.SS "extract"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract [FILE...]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reads the breadcrumb of each file it is given, or of each file \fB.breadcrumbs\fR names, from the file's front matter, and prints it as records other programs can use.
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: the files to read, in the order given. Which files to read is the caller's choice, for example with \fBgit ls\-files\fR or \fBfind\fR; \fBbcr extract\fR does not check the name or the path of a file. Given operands, \fB.breadcrumbs\fR is not read.
.PP
With no operand, \fBbcr extract\fR reads every file that \fB.breadcrumbs\fR, in the current directory, names, in order of path compared as bytes. Run it from the root of the repository. The files are found from the current directory: a directory named \fB.git\fR is not entered, symbolic links are not followed, and only regular files are read.
.PP
\fB.breadcrumbs\fR holds one pattern on each line. Blank lines, and lines that start with \fB#\fR, are ignored:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# the decisions, the specs and the tasks
docs/adrs/*.md
specs/*/spec.md
tasks/*.md
!tasks/README.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
A pattern is matched against the whole path of a file from the current directory, with \fB/\fR between its parts. \fB*\fR matches any characters except \fB/\fR, \fB?\fR one character except \fB/\fR, and \fB[...]\fR one character from a set. Unlike in \fB.gitignore\fR, a pattern with no \fB/\fR names only files at the root: \fB*.md\fR does not name \fBdocs/bcr.md\fR. A pattern that starts with \fB!\fR excludes the files it matches. When several lines match a file, the last one decides; a file no line matches is not read.
.PP
A pattern may not use \fB**\fR, start or end with \fB/\fR, be empty after \fB!\fR, or be malformed, such as with a \fB[\fR that does not close. Such a line is a problem, written to standard error as \fB.breadcrumbs:LINE: MESSAGE\fR and to standard output as a \fBproblem\fR record, and then no file is read.
.PP
Standard input: not read.
.PP
Output: for a file with a valid breadcrumb, one \fBbreadcrumb\fR record on standard output, then one \fBlink\fR record for each entry of its \fBlinks\fR, then one \fBclaim\fR record for each valid entry of its \fBclaims\fR, each in the order written. Fields are separated by a tab:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
breadcrumb	ID	TYPE	PATH	LINE
link	ID	VERB	OBJECT	PATH	LINE
claim	ID	KIND	TARGET	ARGUMENT	PATH	LINE
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBPATH\fR is the file as given, or, with no operand, its path from the current directory, with \fB/\fR between its parts. \fBLINE\fR is the line of the file where the record was written, counted from 1: for a \fBbreadcrumb\fR record, the line of its \fBid\fR; for a \fBlink\fR record, the line of its entry. In a \fBlink\fR record, \fBID\fR is the id of the breadcrumb the link belongs to, and \fBVERB\fR and \fBOBJECT\fR are the two words of its entry.
.PP
In a \fBclaim\fR record, \fBID\fR is the id of the breadcrumb the claim belongs to, and \fBLINE\fR the line of its entry. \fBTARGET\fR, \fBKIND\fR and \fBARGUMENT\fR are the three parts of the entry, without its quotes: \fBTARGET\fR is the file the claim is about, and \fBPATH\fR the file the claim is written in. \fBARGUMENT\fR may hold spaces, but no tab. \fBbcr extract\fR does not open \fBTARGET\fR: a claim about a file that does not exist is printed like any other.
.PP
Select records by their first field, and read their fields by position: new kinds of record may be added, and new fields may be added at the end of a record.
.PP
A file whose first line is not \fB\-\-\-\fR has no front matter, and prints nothing; so does front matter with no \fBbreadcrumb\fR key. A line may end in \fB\en\fR or \fB\er\en\fR.
.PP
A breadcrumb is written under the \fBbreadcrumb\fR key of the front matter, in a small part of YAML: plain one\-line values, lists of them, and \fB[]\fR. It has an \fBid\fR and a \fBtype\fR, each one word, and \fBlinks\fR, a list of entries of a verb and an id. A file whose breadcrumb breaks a rule prints no \fBbreadcrumb\fR, \fBlink\fR or \fBclaim\fR record; each problem is written to standard error, in order of line:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
PATH:LINE: MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each problem is also written to standard output, as a record, so that the commands after \fBbcr extract\fR in a pipe can see it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
problem	PATH	LINE	MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBPATH\fR, \fBLINE\fR and \fBMESSAGE\fR are those written to standard error, with a space in place of any tab or line ending in \fBMESSAGE\fR. A file's \fBproblem\fR records come after its other records, in order of line. A \fBproblem\fR record has no id: it belongs to the breadcrumb whose record has the same \fBPATH\fR, when there is one.
.PP
A breadcrumb may also have \fBclaims\fR, a list of statements about the repository's files. \fBclaims\fR may be left out, and \fBclaims: []\fR means the same; a \fBclaims\fR with no list is a problem like any other, and the file prints no records. Each entry is written on one line, in single quotes, as \fBTARGET KIND ARGUMENT\fR, with a \fB'\fR inside it written \fB''\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
breadcrumb:
  id: verify
  type: spec
  links:
    \- constrained_by ADR\-0016
  claims:
    \- 'docs/bcr.md has\-line ### verify'
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBTARGET\fR is a path from the root of the repository, with \fB/\fR between its parts. The only \fBKIND\fR is \fBhas\-line\fR: its \fBARGUMENT\fR is the text a line of \fBTARGET\fR must equal, once the spaces and tabs at the line's start and end are removed.
.PP
A problem in an entry of \fBclaims\fR drops only that claim: it is written to standard error, the breadcrumb's record, its links and its other claims are still printed, and the exit status is 1. The dropped claim prints no \fBclaim\fR record, only its \fBproblem\fR record. An entry has a problem when:
.IP \(bu 2
it is not in single quotes, has a comment after it on its line, or is written in any other way the breadcrumb's part of YAML does not allow, such as over several lines;
.IP \(bu 2
it has fewer than three parts, or a part is separated from the next by anything other than one space;
.IP \(bu 2
its \fBTARGET\fR starts with \fB/\fR, has a part that is \fB..\fR, or holds white space;
.IP \(bu 2
its \fBKIND\fR is not \fBhas\-line\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
its \fBhas\-line\fR text is empty, starts or ends with a space or a tab, or holds a tab.
.PP
One problem is written for each such entry.
.PP
A file or a directory that cannot be read is written to standard error as a message starting \fBbcr: \fR, and prints no record. The other files are still read.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: every file was read, and none had a problem.
.IP \(bu 2
1: a file had a problem in its breadcrumb, or \fB.breadcrumbs\fR had a problem.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, there is no operand and no \fB.breadcrumbs\fR, or a file could not be read, even when another file had a problem.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract
git ls\-files '*.md' | xargs bcr extract
bcr extract tasks/*.md | grep '^link' | cut \-f2,4
bcr extract | grep '^claim' | cut \-f4,5
bcr extract 2>/dev/null | grep '^problem' | cut \-f2\-
.fi
.RE
.SS "verify"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr verify
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reads the records \fBbcr extract\fR prints, checks the rules about the whole set of breadcrumbs they describe, and passes every record on, with a record for each problem it found:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr verify
.fi
.RE
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: none. \fBbcr verify\fR reads no file that carries a breadcrumb: which files make up the set is decided by \fBbcr extract\fR, from its operands or from \fB.breadcrumbs\fR. The only file it reads is \fBbreadcrumb.rules\fR, in the current directory, when there is one (see The shape of a repository, below).
.PP
Standard input: the records, as \fBbcr extract\fR prints them, read to the end. A line may end in \fB\en\fR or \fB\er\en\fR. Of a \fBclaim\fR record, only its \fBID\fR, \fBPATH\fR and \fBLINE\fR are read. A record of a kind other than \fBbreadcrumb\fR, \fBlink\fR and \fBclaim\fR is not checked, a \fBproblem\fR record included, and neither are fields after the ones \fBbcr extract\fR prints today; both are still copied. With no input, there is no problem.
.PP
Output: standard input, copied to standard output byte for byte. Every line comes out in the order read, with its line ending as read, records of any kind included, so a later command in the pipe gets what \fBbcr extract\fR printed. \fBbcr verify\fR reads its whole input before it writes. It copies the input even when it finds a problem, and prints nothing to standard output when a line is not a record. To see only the problems, send standard output to \fB/dev/null\fR.
.PP
Each problem is written to standard error, in order of \fBPATH\fR compared as bytes, and then of \fBLINE\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
PATH:LINE: MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
After the copy, each problem is also written to standard output, in the same order, as a record like the one \fBbcr extract\fR prints for its own problems:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
problem	PATH	LINE	MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
So the output is equal to the input when no problem is found, and starts with the input when one is. When the last line of the input has no line ending, a \fB\en\fR is written before the first record added.
.PP
\fBPATH\fR and \fBLINE\fR are the ones of the record the problem is about. What is a problem:
.IP \(bu 2
Two or more breadcrumbs with the same id, or with ids that differ only in letter case, such as \fBADR\-0001\fR and \fBadr\-0001\fR. Each of them is a problem, at its \fBbreadcrumb\fR record, and its message names where the others are. Case is compared with Unicode simple case folding.
.IP \(bu 2
A link whose \fBOBJECT\fR is not the id of any breadcrumb in the set, at its \fBlink\fR record. When \fBOBJECT\fR differs only in case from an id, the message names that id.
.PP
A link to an id that two breadcrumbs share is not a problem of its own; the duplicate is.
.PP
The shape of a repository: a repository may declare which types its breadcrumbs have, which links are allowed between them, and which types carry claims, in a file called \fBbreadcrumb.rules\fR at its root. \fBbcr verify\fR reads it from the current directory. With no such file, no shape is checked. Each line is of one of three kinds, with one tab between its fields:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
type	NAME
link	NAME	VERB	NAME
claims	NAME
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtype\fR declares a type a breadcrumb may have.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlink\fR allows a link with \fBVERB\fR from a breadcrumb of the first type to a breadcrumb of the second.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclaims\fR allows a breadcrumb of the type to carry claims.
.PP
A name or a verb is one word, compared as exact bytes: \fBspec\fR and \fBSpec\fR are two types. Lines may come in any order. Blank lines, and lines that start with \fB#\fR, are ignored; no comment follows a field. For example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# decisions above features above changes
type	ADR
type	spec
type	PBI
link	spec	constrained_by	ADR
link	PBI	changes	spec
claims	spec
.fi
.RE
.PP
The shape is closed: what no line allows is a problem, at the record that breaks it.
.IP \(bu 2
A breadcrumb whose type no \fBtype\fR line declares, at its \fBbreadcrumb\fR record: \fBtype "Spec" is not declared in breadcrumb.rules\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A link when no \fBlink\fR line has the type of the breadcrumb it is written in, its verb, and the type of the breadcrumb it points to, at its \fBlink\fR record: \fBlink "PBI implements ADR" matches no rule in breadcrumb.rules\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A claim of a breadcrumb whose type has no \fBclaims\fR line, at its \fBclaim\fR record: \fBa breadcrumb of type "PBI" may not carry claims under breadcrumb.rules\fR.
.PP
One cause is one problem. A link or a claim of a breadcrumb whose type is not declared is not judged, and neither is a link to such a breadcrumb, a link that points nowhere, or a link from or to an id that two breadcrumbs share. The rules say which links are allowed, not which are required: a breadcrumb with no link is not a problem.
.PP
A line \fBbreadcrumb.rules\fR does not allow is a problem too, written as \fBbreadcrumb.rules:LINE: MESSAGE\fR and as a \fBproblem\fR record whose \fBPATH\fR is \fBbreadcrumb.rules\fR:
.IP \(bu 2
a line of a kind other than \fBtype\fR, \fBlink\fR and \fBclaims\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
a line with the wrong number of fields: 2 for \fBtype\fR and \fBclaims\fR, 4 for \fBlink\fR. A line typed with spaces between its fields is told to use a tab;
.IP \(bu 2
a field that is empty or holds white space;
.IP \(bu 2
a \fBlink\fR or \fBclaims\fR line that names a type no \fBtype\fR line declares;
.IP \(bu 2
a line that repeats an earlier one.
.PP
While \fBbreadcrumb.rules\fR has a problem, the shape is not known, and nothing is judged against it; ids and links are still checked as above. A file with no \fBtype\fR line, an empty one included, is a shape with no type: every breadcrumb is then a problem. Deleting the file turns the shape checks off.
.PP
A line of input that is not a record \fBbcr extract\fR could print, an empty line included, stops \fBbcr verify\fR: it writes a message starting \fBbcr: \fR, which gives the line's number in the input, and prints no problem.
.PP
A breadcrumb \fBbcr extract\fR could not read has no record, so links to its id are reported as pointing to no breadcrumb. Fix the problem \fBbcr extract\fR printed first.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: no problem was found.
.IP \(bu 2
1: at least one problem was found.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr verify\fR was given an operand, its input could not be read, a line of its input was not a record, \fBbreadcrumb.rules\fR exists and could not be read, or its output could not be written.
.PP
In a pipe, the shell returns the exit status of \fBbcr verify\fR, so a problem \fBbcr extract\fR found is seen only on standard error. A script that needs both runs with \fBset \-o pipefail\fR, or saves the records first.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr verify > /dev/null
set \-o pipefail; bcr extract | bcr verify > /dev/null
bcr extract > records.tsv && bcr verify < records.tsv | cmp \- records.tsv
.fi
.RE
.SS "audit"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr audit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reads the records \fBbcr extract\fR and \fBbcr verify\fR print, checks each claim against the file it is about, and passes every record on, with a verdict for each claim and each breadcrumb:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: none. Run \fBbcr audit\fR from the root of the repository: the file a claim is about is found from the current directory.
.PP
Standard input: the records, as \fBbcr extract\fR and \fBbcr verify\fR print them, read to the end. A line may end in \fB\en\fR or \fB\er\en\fR. \fBbcr audit\fR reads the \fBbreadcrumb\fR, \fBclaim\fR and \fBproblem\fR records. A record of another kind, a \fBlink\fR record included, is not checked, and neither are fields after the ones printed today; both are still copied. With no input, nothing is printed.
.PP
A verdict is \fBConfirmed\fR, \fBRefuted\fR or \fBUndecided\fR.
.PP
The verdict of a claim: a \fBhas\-line\fR claim is \fBConfirmed\fR when its \fBTARGET\fR is a regular file and one of its lines, without its line ending and without the spaces and tabs at its start and end, is equal to the claim's text, byte for byte. Otherwise it is \fBRefuted\fR: so is a claim whose \fBTARGET\fR does not exist, or is a directory, a symbolic link, or anything else that is not a regular file. A symbolic link is not followed. The files are read as they are on disk, uncommitted changes included.
.PP
The verdict of a breadcrumb comes from its own claims, and from its problems, the \fBproblem\fR records with its \fBPATH\fR:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRefuted\fR, when at least one of its claims is \fBRefuted\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
otherwise \fBUndecided\fR, when it has a problem, such as a claim \fBbcr extract\fR dropped;
.IP \(bu 2
otherwise \fBConfirmed\fR, when it has at least one claim;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUndecided\fR, with the reason \fBno claims\fR, when it has no claim and no problem: nothing was checked, so nothing is confirmed.
.PP
No link carries a verdict from one breadcrumb to another.
.PP
Output: standard input, copied to standard output byte for byte, as \fBbcr verify\fR does. After it, one \fBclaim\-verdict\fR record for each \fBclaim\fR record, in the order read, then one \fBverdict\fR record for each \fBbreadcrumb\fR record, in the order read. Fields are separated by a tab:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
claim\-verdict	ID	VERDICT	PATH	LINE
verdict	ID	VERDICT	PATH	LINE
verdict	ID	Undecided	PATH	LINE	no claims
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBID\fR, \fBPATH\fR and \fBLINE\fR are those of the \fBclaim\fR or \fBbreadcrumb\fR record the verdict is about, so two breadcrumbs with the same id each get their own. A \fBverdict\fR record has a sixth field only for a breadcrumb with no claims. \fBbcr audit\fR reads its whole input, and every file the claims are about, before it writes. When the last line of the input has no line ending, a \fB\en\fR is written before the first record added.
.PP
Each \fBRefuted\fR claim is written to standard error, in the order read, at the \fBPATH\fR and \fBLINE\fR of its \fBclaim\fR record. The message names the file and what it lacks:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
PATH:LINE: MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
A line of input that is not a record \fBbcr extract\fR could print stops \fBbcr audit\fR: it writes a message starting \fBbcr: \fR, which gives the line's number in the input, and prints nothing to standard output. So does a \fBclaim\fR record whose \fBTARGET\fR starts with \fB/\fR or has a part that is \fB..\fR, whose \fBKIND\fR is not \fBhas\-line\fR, or whose text no line can equal. A regular file that cannot be read stops it in the same way.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: no claim is \fBRefuted\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
1: at least one claim is \fBRefuted\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr audit\fR was given an operand, its input could not be read, a line of its input was not a record, a file a claim is about could not be read, or its output could not be written.
.PP
An \fBUndecided\fR breadcrumb does not change the exit status. A problem \fBbcr extract\fR or \fBbcr verify\fR found fails the pipe only with \fBset \-o pipefail\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
set \-o pipefail; bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit > /dev/null
bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit 2>/dev/null | grep '^verdict' | cut \-f2,3
bcr extract | bcr audit | grep \-w Refuted
.fi
.RE
.SS "report"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr report
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reads the records \fBbcr extract\fR, \fBbcr verify\fR and \fBbcr audit\fR print, and writes one page that shows them: every breadcrumb with its verdict, every problem, every claim, and one diagram for each spec. It is the last command of the pipe, and does not pass the records on:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit | bcr report > report.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: none. \fBbcr report\fR reads no file of the repository.
.PP
Standard input: the records, read to the end. A line may end in \fB\en\fR or \fB\er\en\fR. \fBbcr report\fR reads the \fBbreadcrumb\fR, \fBlink\fR, \fBclaim\fR, \fBproblem\fR, \fBclaim\-verdict\fR and \fBverdict\fR records. A record of another kind is not read, and neither are fields after the ones printed today.
.PP
Output: one page, in Markdown, on standard output. Its diagrams are Mermaid, in fenced blocks marked \fBmermaid\fR; GitHub draws them wherever it shows Markdown. The page has four sections, in this order:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB## Breadcrumbs\fR: a line of counts, then a table with one row for each breadcrumb, with its verdict, its id, its type and where it is written, as \fBPATH:LINE\fR. Rows are in order of verdict, \fBRefuted\fR first, then \fBUndecided\fR, \fBConfirmed\fR and \fBnot audited\fR, and then of id.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB## Problems\fR: one item for each problem \fBbcr extract\fR and \fBbcr verify\fR printed, as \fBPATH:LINE: MESSAGE\fR, in order of \fBPATH\fR and then of \fBLINE\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB## Claims\fR: a table with one row for each claim, with its verdict, the id of its breadcrumb, the claim as it is written, and where it is written. Rows are in the same order as the breadcrumbs.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB## Views\fR: one diagram for each breadcrumb whose type is \fBspec\fR, under a heading with its id, in order of id.
.PP
A section with nothing to show says so, as in \fBNo problems.\fR.
.PP
A view draws the spec, every breadcrumb with a link to it, every breadcrumb it has a link to, and the links among those. A breadcrumb two links away is not in it, and a breadcrumb in no view is still in the table. Each box shows a breadcrumb's id, its type and its verdict, and each of its claims after the claim's own verdict. Each arrow goes from the breadcrumb a link is written in to the one it points to, and is labelled with the link's verb.
.PP
A verdict is shown as \fBbcr audit\fR printed it: \fBConfirmed\fR, \fBRefuted\fR, \fBUndecided\fR, or \fBUndecided, no claims\fR. \fBbcr report\fR judges nothing. A breadcrumb or a claim with no verdict in the input is shown as \fBnot audited\fR, so the page can be made without \fBbcr audit\fR.
.PP
The same records give the same page, byte for byte: it holds no date, and its rows, boxes and arrows are in order of id, whatever the order of the records.
.PP
An id, a path, a verb, a claim or a message is written so that it cannot change the page around it. In the tables and lists, a \fB\e\fR comes before each character Markdown reads as syntax, such as \fB|\fR, `\fB \fR `\fB and \fR<\fB. In a diagram, a box is named \fRn1\fB, \fRn2\fB and so on, never by an id, and in its label every character Mermaid could read as syntax is written as a code, such as \fR#35;\fB for \fR#\fB. \fRbcr report\fB writes no \fRclick`, no link and no directive into a diagram.
.PP
A line of input that is not a record the pipe could print stops \fBbcr report\fR: it writes a message starting \fBbcr: \fR, which gives the line's number in the input, and prints no page. So does a \fBverdict\fR or \fBclaim\-verdict\fR record whose verdict is not \fBConfirmed\fR, \fBRefuted\fR or \fBUndecided\fR.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: the page was written, whatever the verdicts and the problems.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr report\fR was given an operand, its input could not be read, a line of its input was not a record, or its output could not be written.
.PP
A \fBRefuted\fR claim does not change the exit status of \fBbcr report\fR. It fails the pipe through \fBbcr audit\fR, under \fBset \-o pipefail\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit | bcr report > report.md
set \-o pipefail; bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit | bcr report > report.md
bcr extract | bcr report
.fi
.RE
.SS "filter"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr filter [\-i ID] [\-o ID] [\-t TYPE] [\-k KIND]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reads the records of the pipe, and prints only those that match the flags it is given, unchanged. It can stand wherever records flow. Right after \fBbcr extract\fR, it looks a breadcrumb up:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr filter \-\-id ADR\-0019
.fi
.RE
.PP
After \fBbcr audit\fR, it shows one breadcrumb's verdict, judged with the whole set:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit | bcr filter \-\-id ADR\-0019
.fi
.RE
.PP
Flags:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-i ID\fR, \fB\-\-id ID\fR: a \fBbreadcrumb\fR, \fBlink\fR, \fBclaim\fR, \fBclaim\-verdict\fR or \fBverdict\fR record matches when its \fBID\fR field equals \fBID\fR. A \fBproblem\fR record has no \fBID\fR field, and never matches.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-o ID\fR, \fB\-\-object ID\fR: a \fBlink\fR record matches when its \fBOBJECT\fR field, the id the link points to, equals \fBID\fR. No other kind of record matches.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-t TYPE\fR, \fB\-\-type TYPE\fR: a \fBbreadcrumb\fR record matches when its \fBTYPE\fR field equals \fBTYPE\fR. No other kind of record matches.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-k KIND\fR, \fB\-\-kind KIND\fR: a record matches when its first field equals \fBKIND\fR, whether or not \fBbcr\fR knows that kind.
.PP
At least one flag is given. Each flag may be given more than once: a record matches a flag when it matches at least one of its values, and is printed when it matches every flag given. Fields are compared byte for byte, letter case included, so \fBadr\-0019\fR does not select \fBADR\-0019\fR.
.PP
Operands: none. \fBbcr filter\fR reads no file.
.PP
Standard input: the records, as \fBbcr extract\fR, \fBbcr verify\fR and \fBbcr audit\fR print them, read to the end. A line may end in \fB\en\fR or \fB\er\en\fR. A record of a kind \fBbcr\fR does not know matches only \fB\-\-kind\fR, and fields after the ones printed today are not read.
.PP
Output: each record that matches, on standard output, as it was read, byte for byte, with its line ending, in the order read. \fBbcr filter\fR adds no record and changes none. It reads its whole input before it writes. When no record matches, it prints nothing, and that is not an error.
.PP
\fBbcr filter\fR judges nothing: two records with the same id both match, and a claim or a verdict is not checked. So it comes after the commands that judge, not before them. Before \fBbcr verify\fR, a link to a breadcrumb filtered out is reported as pointing nowhere; before \fBbcr audit\fR, a \fBproblem\fR record that is dropped can no longer make its breadcrumb \fBUndecided\fR.
.PP
A \fBproblem\fR record is printed only with \fB\-\-kind problem\fR: it has no id, so the problems of a breadcrumb do not follow it. \fB\-\-type\fR alone prints \fBbreadcrumb\fR records only; the links and claims of those breadcrumbs are found by their ids, with a second \fBbcr filter\fR.
.PP
A link is written only in the breadcrumb it belongs to: \fB\-\-id X\fR selects the links X writes, and \fB\-\-object X\fR the links that point to X, wherever they are written. In what \fBbcr extract\fR prints, which has no link from a breadcrumb to itself, \fB\-\-id X \-\-object X\fR selects nothing, so the two directions are two runs over the same records:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract > records.tsv
bcr filter \-\-kind link \-\-id ADR\-0019 < records.tsv
bcr filter \-\-object ADR\-0019 < records.tsv
.fi
.RE
.PP
A line of input that is not a record the pipe could print, an empty line included, stops \fBbcr filter\fR: it writes a message starting \fBbcr: \fR, which gives the line's number in the input, and prints no record. A record of a kind the pipe prints needs the fields of its kind, none of them empty, and a \fBLINE\fR that is a whole number from 1.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: the input was read to its end, whether or not a record matched.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr filter\fR was given an operand or no flag, its input could not be read, a line of its input was not a record, or its output could not be written.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr extract | bcr filter \-\-id ADR\-0019
bcr extract | bcr filter \-\-type ADR | wc \-l
bcr extract | bcr filter \-k link \-i PBI\-00001 | cut \-f3,4
bcr extract | bcr filter \-\-object ADR\-0019 | cut \-f2,3
bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit | bcr filter \-\-kind verdict \-\-id filter
bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr filter \-\-kind problem | cut \-f2\-
.fi
.RE
.SS "init"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr init [\-a FILE]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sets up the repository whose root is the current directory for the pipe. It writes no breadcrumb: which files carry breadcrumbs, and what they are, is the repository's choice. Each of three pieces is set up only when it is not there yet, found by its name:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.breadcrumbs\fR, holding only comments: what a pattern is, and example patterns, commented out. Until a pattern is written in it, \fBbcr extract\fR reads no file, and the pipe exits 0 with a page that says \fBNo breadcrumbs.\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A \fB## Breadcrumbs\fR section for agents in the agents file, saying where breadcrumbs live, to run the pipe before committing, and never to edit a claim to turn a \fBRefuted\fR verdict green, then showing how to look breadcrumbs up with \fBbcr filter\fR: one by its id, every one of a type, what links to one, and one's verdict. It is added at the end of the file, after an empty line, when the file has no line \fB## Breadcrumbs\fR; a file that is not there is written with the section alone.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.github/workflows/breadcrumbs.yml\fR, a GitHub Actions workflow that, on each push and pull request, installs this release of \fBbcr\fR from its Linux archive, checked against the release's checksums, runs the pipe under \fBset \-o pipefail\fR, and puts the page \fBbcr report\fR writes in the job's summary and in an artifact of the run.
.PP
Flags:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-a FILE\fR, \fB\-\-agents\-file FILE\fR: the agents file, a path from the root of the repository, such as \fBCLAUDE.md\fR. Without it, \fBAGENTS.md\fR. A path that starts with \fB/\fR or has a part that is \fB..\fR is a usage error.
.PP
Operands: none.
.PP
Standard input: not read.
.PP
Output: on standard output, the path of each file written or added to, one per line, in the order above. On standard error, a message for each piece that was already there, and is left as it is.
.PP
\fBbcr init\fR replaces no file, and changes no file but the agents file, by adding its section at the end. It finds what to write before it writes anything. The workflow installs the release of \fBbcr\fR that wrote it, so \fBbcr init\fR needs a released \fBbcr\fR: one built with \fBgo run\fR, or with no release version, exits 2 and writes nothing.
.PP
On a CI other than GitHub Actions, the same check is:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
set \-o pipefail; bcr extract | bcr verify | bcr audit > /dev/null
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: every piece is in place, set up now or before.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr init\fR was used wrongly, this \fBbcr\fR has no release version, the agents file is not a regular file, or a file could not be read or written.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr init
bcr init \-a CLAUDE.md
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
Every command exits with (ADR\-0006):
.IP \(bu 2
0: it succeeded and found nothing wrong.
.IP \(bu 2
1: it ran and found something wrong in the breadcrumbs.
.IP \(bu 2
2: it was used wrongly, or could not run.
.PP
\fBbcr report\fR, \fBbcr filter\fR and \fBbcr init\fR judge nothing, so they exit only with 0 or 2.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
The method: \fBREADME.md\fR. How the repository is developed: \fBCONTRIBUTING.md\fR.
